public class SecureConnectionManager {

    private ExecutorService executorService;
    private SSLSocketFactory sslSocketFactory;

    public SecureConnectionManager() {
        try {
            SSLContext sslContext = SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
            sslContext.init(null, null, new java.security.SecureRandom());
            this.sslSocketFactory = sslContext.getSocketFactory();
            this.executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException | KeyManagementException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to initialize SSL context", e);
        }
    }

    public void connectToServer(String host, int port, int timeout) throws IOException {
        executorService.submit(() -> {
            try (SSLSocket socket = (SSLSocket) sslSocketFactory.createSocket(host, port)) {
                socket.setSoTimeout(timeout);
                // Perform connection logic here
            } catch (IOException e) {
                if (e instanceof SocketTimeoutException) {
                    System.out.println("Connection timed out. Retrying...");
                    connectToServer(host, port, timeout); // Retry mechanism
                } else {
                    throw new RuntimeException("Failed to establish SSL/TLS connection", e);
                }
            }
        });
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        executorService.shutdown();
    }
}
```

This code snippet provides a basic structure for managing secure connections using SSL/TLS in Java. It includes an executor service for handling concurrent connections and a retry mechanism for transient failures. The actual connection logic should be implemented within the try-with-resources block where the `SSLSocket` is created and used.']

